@@s001 Non-linear boundaries, SVM and trees
@@s002 dato:inner class,due:ring,no:circle r = 3,ok:better boundary
@@s003 One point out of four of the dataset (code of the notebook, my seed). No line separates the inner cloud from the ring: the linear classifier reaches 0.519.
@@s004 Circle of radius 3, as in the notebook: accuracy <b>0.744</b>. Half of the points of the ring fall inside.
@@s005 With radius 1.92 the accuracy rises to <b>0.971</b>: the circle passes through the empty zone between the two classes.
@@s006 Now the same points in <b>polar coordinates</b>: horizontally the angle θ, vertically the distance r from the centre. The two classes become two horizontal bands.
@@s007 In this space the boundary is a <b>line</b>: r = 1.92. A linear classifier on the right features solves a non-linear problem.
@@s008 dato:class +1,due:class −1,ok:boundary and margins,ora:support vectors
@@s009 Six points, two classes. Many lines separate them: this grey one, for example, passes very close to a point.
@@s010 The SVM chooses w = [0.5, 0.5], b = 0: the boundary is x₂ = −x₁.
@@s011 The dashed lines are wᵀx + b = +1 and = −1: the edges of the "road". No point lies inside it.
@@s012 The <b>support vectors</b> (1, 1) and (−1, −1) lie exactly on the edges: y(wᵀx + b) = 1. Only they decide where the boundary passes.
@@s013 Width of the road: 2 / ‖w‖ = 2 / 0.707 = <b>2.83</b>, that is the distance between the two support vectors. The SVM is the line with the widest road.
@@s014 due:class 1 (inner cloud),dato:class 2 (ring),ok:circle of radius r,no:a line
@@s015 due:class 1,dato:class 2,ok:thresholds of the tree
@@s016 Non-linear boundaries, SVM and trees
@@s017 Exercise of week 10 <i>Non-linear decision boundaries</i> and lecture 10 (SVM and decision trees). When a line is not enough: transform the features, maximise the margin, split the space into pieces. Exam question <b>Q8 (c)</b>. DW chapters 14, 16, 17.
@@s018 The ring dataset
@@s019 Class 1: a cloud around the origin. Class 2: a ring of radius about 3 around it. No line can separate them: the linear classifier with least squares reaches <b>0.519</b>, almost like tossing a coin.
@@s020 Radius of the circle (Tasks 2–4)
@@s021 accuracy
@@s022 inner ones right
@@s023 ring ones right
@@s024 Code of the notebook with my seed (<code>np.random.seed(0)</code>); with r = 3 it gives 0.744 as in the notebook. Circle too small: many inner points end up "outside" (errors on one class); too large: many points of the ring end up "inside". The radius shifts the errors from one class to the other: it is the exchange between false positives and false negatives.
@@s025 No line, but a circle yes
@@s026 Changing the features
@@s027 The circle becomes a line in another space
@@s028 The circular boundary. It is not linear in x and y…
@@s029 …but it is <b>linear in the weights</b> if one uses the quadratic features: the same trick as the <a href="w04-polinomi.html">polynomials</a> and the <a href="w05-kernel.html">kernels</a>.
@@s030 The circle finds it by itself, without being told the shape.
@@s031 Task 5: with the right feature a threshold on a single number is enough. The preprocessing can turn a non-linear problem into a linear one. The difference from a kernel: here you choose the transformation by hand, knowing that the data are circular.
@@s032 From the circle to the line, changing coordinates
@@s033 Support Vector Machine
@@s034 The widest margin
@@s035 The SVM chooses the one with the widest "road" between the two classes (large margin).
@@s036 Only the points on the edge of the road decide the boundary. The others, even very far away, do not count: for this reason a distant outlier weighs little (unlike least squares).
@@s037 Maximising the margin = minimising ‖w‖: it is also a regularisation.
@@s038 Support vectors (1, 1) and (−1, −1), with y(wᵀx + b) = 1; the other 4 points have values between 1.75 and 2.25. Computed with <code>SVC(kernel="linear")</code>.
@@s039 The widest road
@@s040 Weights and support vectors from <code>SVC(kernel="linear")</code>.
@@s041 The loss of the SVM (DW 17)
@@s042 <span class="m">L(w) = ½‖w‖² + C · Σᵢ max(0, 1 − yᵢ(wᵀxᵢ + b))</span>. The second term is the <b>hinge loss</b>: 0 for the well-classified points outside the road, it grows for those inside the road or on the wrong side. <b>C</b> large: few errors tolerated, narrow margin (risk of overfitting). C small: wide margin, some errors accepted.
@@s043 Kernel trick
@@s044 The prediction is written as a sum over the support vectors: <span class="m">f(x) = Σᵢ αᵢ yᵢ K(x, xᵢ) + b</span>. Putting a <b>kernel</b> K (for example RBF, Gaussian) in place of the inner product xᵀxᵢ, the SVM traces curved boundaries without building the features by hand. On the ring: linear SVM 0.66 (with 788 support vectors out of 800), RBF SVM <b>0.975</b> with 103 support vectors.
@@s045 Decision trees (lecture 10)
@@s046 A tree asks one question at a time on a feature ("x &lt; 1.3?") and divides the space into rectangles; each leaf gives a class. On the ring:
@@s047 Maximum depth
@@s048 unlimited
@@s049 accuracy (training)
@@s050 leaves
@@s051 <code>DecisionTreeClassifier</code> of sklearn. Without a depth limit the tree isolates every point: accuracy 1.0 on the training, but it is memory, not generalisation. The depth is its measure of complexity, like the degree of a polynomial. The boundary is made of steps parallel to the axes.
@@s052 Q8 (c)
@@s053 "Linear and non-linear decision boundaries including SVM … and possibly HOG features." To bring: why a line fails on the ring, how the features (quadratic, polar, HOG) make the problem linear, margin and support vectors, hinge loss and C, kernel trick, and the comparison with trees and k-NN.
@@s054 Thresholds on one feature at a time
